---
/**
 * CopyPlusStatsBlock — the `copy_plus_stats` layout: the hubs' opening "why"
 * band. Copy in the left column, a stack of stat cards in the right.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. Three live hub pages ship this exact shape and this
 * component is their union, element for element:
 *
 *   src/pages/implant-dentistry/index.astro:276-310   `#why`
 *   src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:236-279  `#why`
 *   src/pages/general-dentistry/index.astro:202-251   `#why`
 *
 * `.why-grid` / `.why-copy` / `.why-stats` / `.stat-card` are the pages' own
 * class names on purpose, the same reason CardGridBlock keeps `.why-card`: the
 * three page stylesheets still carry rules for them and keep carrying them
 * until the stylesheet pass removes their copies, so renaming anything here to
 * `vs-copy-stats__*` would unstyle the band the moment an editor's backfill
 * filled `blocks`.
 *
 * BEWARE THE NAME COLLISION. `.why-grid` here is NOT CardGridBlock's
 * `.why-grid`. That one is a grid OF CARDS (`article.why-card`, 2-4 columns);
 * this one is the two-column copy-beside-stats split (`1.15fr 1fr`). Same
 * class, two unrelated layouts, on sixteen and three pages respectively — which
 * is exactly why each block states its own geometry in its own scoped <style>
 * instead of leaning on a shared sheet. Do not "unify" them.
 *
 * THE THREE PAGES ARE NOT UNIFORM, and the differences are what the fields are:
 *
 *   - implant and general open the second half of the copy with an `<h3>`
 *     ("Robotic-precision implant placement", "Advanced technology for
 *     accurate, comfortable care"); cosmetic has a second paragraph and NO
 *     heading. So `body2Heading` and `body2` are independent — filling one
 *     never conditions the other, and cosmetic's row must keep emitting a bare
 *     second `<p>`.
 *   - implant's third card is `<div class="stat-num">0</div>` with no `<em>`;
 *     the others carry `600<em>+</em>`, `300<em>+</em>`, `1<em>st</em>`. So
 *     `unit` is optional and renders no `<em>` when blank rather than an empty
 *     one, set tight against the figure with no space — the same rule
 *     StatCalloutBlock applies to `.lasting-stat .big`.
 *   - exactly one card per page — the 300+ reviews card — opens its detail with
 *     a decorative star run. So `stars` is per-row, and rows that do not set it
 *     emit no `<span>`, no space and no `&nbsp;`.
 *
 * THE STAR RUN, byte for byte. All three pages ship
 *
 *     <span class="stars" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span> &nbsp;Consistent…
 *
 * — an `aria-hidden` span (the rating is already stated in words by
 * `.stat-label`, so a screen reader that also read five black stars would say
 * it twice), then a PLAIN SPACE, then a NON-BREAKING SPACE, then the detail. The
 * `&nbsp;` is the visible gap between the stars and the sentence; the plain
 * space in front of it is the source's own and survives into the build
 * (dist/implant-dentistry/index.html, verified). Both are carried. `&nbsp;` is
 * emitted as the entity rather than as a literal U+00A0 through an expression,
 * so the bytes match the pages this replaces and not merely the words.
 *
 * `stars` IS A FLAG, NOT COPY — and reading it as copy is the defect this
 * revision closes. `stats.stars` is registered as a `true_false`
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-content-model.php, `field_vs_blk_cstats_stat_stars`), so
 * wpgraphql-acf returns a BOOLEAN. This file used to run it through `text()`,
 * which returns "" for anything that is not a string — so `stars: true` fell to
 * the blank branch and the span, the plain space and the `&nbsp;` all drew
 * NOTHING, on every one of the three hubs. Nobody caught it because the
 * word-level diff that grades this migration cannot see it: five glyphs in an
 * `aria-hidden` span are decoration, not words, so a band that loses the entire
 * run still measures lossless. Only a class-level diff shows `span.stars`
 * missing. Rendered, not reasoned about: harness cases 01-10 in the scratchpad.
 *
 * THE GLYPHS ARE THE COMPONENT'S, NOT THE FIELD'S. The editor says whether the
 * run appears; this template owns what it is. That is deliberate and it is
 * MediaSplitBlock's contract for `creds.stars` already (:156-162, :822) — the
 * run is `aria-hidden` presentation, so storing ★★★★★ in a text field would put
 * it somewhere a retype can mangle it into four stars, or into an accessible
 * name that reads "black star black star black star…". Printing the field's
 * value would also re-open exactly that door.
 *
 * WHICH IS WHY `flag()` ACCEPTS MORE THAN A BOOLEAN. The route maps written
 * against the old broken behaviour put the five literal glyphs in this field as
 * a STRING (cms/import/routes/general-dentistry.json), and the sibling maps put
 * `true`. The data is being corrected, but this component must be right under
 * either, and under the "0"/"1" strings ACF stores a true_false as natively —
 * because "0" is a non-empty string, and a truthiness test that only asked
 * `Boolean(value)` would turn the OFF toggle ON. Named failure mode, guarded
 * below.
 *
 * ONE BUTTON, NOT TWO. Each of the three pages ships a single ghost button at
 * the foot of `.why-copy` — `#doctors`, `#doctors`, `/about-us/` — with no
 * `.cta-row` wrapper of any kind: the button is a direct child of `.why-copy`
 * and the 28px gap above it comes from `.why-copy p:last-of-type`. A second
 * pair of CTA fields is deliberately NOT offered. Adding one would need a
 * wrapper that no page in this family has, which is designing, and this phase
 * does not design.
 *
 * THE VARIANT IS THE LITERAL `ghost`, not derived from the band the way
 * CardGridBlock derives `sage`/`light`. Two reasons, both measurable: all three
 * pages spell `variant="ghost"` on a charcoal band, so deriving would emit
 * `vs-btn-ghost-light` and change the class attribute on all three; and
 * Button.astro:342 already carries a charcoal-band override that repaints
 * `ghost` white inside `.section.alt`, so the derived variant would buy nothing
 * but a diff. If this layout is ever put on a `sage` band — no page in the
 * corpus does — the variant question reopens as a designed change, here, not as
 * a guess today.
 *
 * HREF POLICY: `ctaHref` stores an anchor ("#doctors"), a site path
 * ("/about-us/") or one of the tokens book|phone|map, resolved through the ONE
 * shared copy of the token map and hover table in ./cta.ts. Never a pasted
 * booking/tel/maps URL — those live in src/data/contact.ts so they cannot go
 * stale in a CMS field.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS. An editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158), so every field is read as if it might
 * be missing, blank, or the wrong type.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5. Every rule below names the
 * sheet and line it was lifted from; the page sheets' local `--sage` / `--ink` /
 * `--f-display` aliases are resolved back to the `--vs-*` tokens they alias
 * one-for-one (implant-dentistry.css:28-44), so rendered values are unchanged.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.2, 2.5, 3.3.
 */

import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { resolveCta } from "./cta";

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class this block writes on its own section.
 *
 * Read this map, never an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on
 * these three hubs and cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 (Fact 4), so a block
 * that inherited its band would be wrong on one of them the moment a section
 * moved. `paper` maps to nothing at all because a paper band is a bare
 * `class="section"` on every page in the corpus — adding a modifier would be
 * the diff.
 */
const BAND_MODIFIER: Record<string, string | null> = {
  paper: null,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

/** One row of the layout's `stats` repeater — one `.stat-card`. */
export interface CopyPlusStatsRow {
  /** The figure, as written: "600", "300", "1", "0". Text, not a number. */
  value?: string | null;
  /** The `<em>` set tight against the figure: "+", "st". Blank emits no tag. */
  unit?: string | null;
  /** The uppercase line under the figure. */
  label?: string | null;
  /** The sentence under that line. */
  detail?: string | null;
  /**
   * ON/OFF for the decorative star run in front of `detail` — set on the one
   * card per page that carries it. A `true_false` in the schema, so the wire
   * type is `boolean`; the string and number forms are accepted only so the
   * component survives the maps written against the old broken behaviour and
   * ACF's own "0"/"1" storage. See the header, and `flag()` below.
   *
   * The glyphs themselves are NOT stored here — the template owns them, and
   * marks them `aria-hidden` because `label` already says "Five-Star Google
   * Reviews" in words. See the header for the two spaces that follow the span.
   */
  stars?: boolean | string | number | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** The first paragraph, under the heading. */
  body?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `<h3>` that opens the second half of the copy. Independent of `body2`:
   * cosmetic ships the paragraph without the heading, so a blank value here
   * must emit no element rather than an empty `<h3>`.
   */
  body2Heading?: string | null;
  /**
   * The paragraph under that heading — or under `body`, when there is no
   * heading. A field of its own and not a longer `body`, per the house rule
   * already applied to media_split's `body_2`: two paragraphs folded into one
   * `<p>` are the same words in the wrong markup.
   */
  body2?: string | null;
  /**
   * The `<h3>` opening the THIRD block of copy, and its paragraph. Same
   * independence rule as the pair above: /emergency-dentistry/ `#when` fills
   * both, every other saved row fills neither, and a blank value must emit no
   * element. Without these the band drops a real `<h3>` and ~50 words.
   */
  body3Heading?: string | null;
  /** Mirrors `body2` exactly, including the escaping — see the render. */
  body3?: string | null;
  stats?: (CopyPlusStatsRow | null)[] | null;
  /** The single ghost button. See the header for why there is only one. */
  ctaLabel?: string | null;
  ctaHref?: string | null;
  /** Hover override. Blank falls back to the destination table in ./cta.ts. */
  ctaHover?: string | null;
}

const {
  id,
  band,
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  body2Heading,
  body2,
  body3Heading,
  body3,
  stats,
  ctaLabel,
  ctaHref,
  ctaHover,
} = Astro.props;

/* `?? null` rather than a default: an unknown band from a future PHP choice
   falls through to the paper shell rather than emitting a class with no rule. */
const bandModifier = BAND_MODIFIER[band ?? "paper"] ?? null;

/** Trim once, here, so every guard below tests for real content rather than
 *  for a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

/**
 * Read an ACF `true_false` as the on/off it is, whatever shape it arrives in.
 *
 * `text()` is the wrong reader for this field and was the defect: it returns ""
 * for a boolean, so `stars: true` — the ONLY value the live schema can send —
 * silently meant "off". This is the whole of the fix.
 *
 * The two string cases are the ones that bite, and neither is hypothetical:
 *
 *   - "★★★★★". The route maps drafted against the broken component wrote the
 *     literal glyphs into this boolean field to force the run to appear
 *     (cms/import/routes/general-dentistry.json). Any non-empty word means ON,
 *     so those rows keep working while the data is corrected — and they render
 *     the template's glyph run, not the pasted value, so a map that typed four
 *     stars still gets five.
 *   - "0". ACF stores a true_false as the STRINGS "0" and "1", and a raw REST
 *     or wp-json read returns them that way. `Boolean("0")` is `true`, so a
 *     naive truthiness test would turn every OFF toggle ON — the loudest
 *     possible failure, five stars on all three cards of all three hubs. "0"
 *     and "false" are therefore spelled out as off.
 *
 * Everything that is not a boolean, a number or a string — `null` from a
 * toggle an editor never touched, `undefined` from a field nobody queried —
 * is off. `null` must mean "no stars", not "maybe"; MediaSplitBlock:645-647
 * says the same thing about the same field on the same grounds.
 */
const flag = (input: unknown): boolean => {
  if (typeof input === "boolean") return input;
  if (typeof input === "number") return input !== 0;
  if (typeof input !== "string") return false;
  const value = input.trim().toLowerCase();
  return value !== "" && value !== "0" && value !== "false";
};

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const body2HeadingText = text(body2Heading);
const body2Text = text(body2);
const body3HeadingText = text(body3Heading);
const body3Text = text(body3);

/* The entity, not the character. `set:html` inserts this string raw, so the
   build emits `&nbsp;` exactly as the three templates wrote it; interpolating
   " " through a normal expression would emit the raw byte pair instead —
   the same word to a reader, a different byte to the diff that grades this. */
const NBSP_HTML = "&nbsp;";

/**
 * `Array.isArray` first because an unqueried repeater arrives `undefined` and
 * one an editor never opened arrives `null`, and `.map` on either throws
 * during the build of whatever page the band sits on.
 *
 * The filter drops only the wholly blank row ACF leaves behind after an
 * abandoned "Add a stat" — a row with ANY part filled is a row an editor
 * meant, and drawing a card with a label and no figure is better than silently
 * deleting the label.
 *
 * `stars` is deliberately NOT one of the fields that keeps a row alive, and it
 * is the one exception to "any part filled". It is a toggle, not content: a
 * row whose only mark is a clicked toggle is the leftover of an abandoned "Add
 * a stat", and honouring it would put a card on a live hub containing nothing
 * but a floating star run — five sage glyphs over empty space, with no figure
 * and no label to say what was rated. MediaSplitBlock:643-647 drops its
 * stars-only cred row for exactly this reason, and dropping it here also keeps
 * the fix additive: a stars-only row renders nothing today (because `text()`
 * blanked the flag) and must still render nothing.
 */
const rows = (Array.isArray(stats) ? stats : [])
  .map((row) => ({
    value: text(row?.value),
    unit: text(row?.unit),
    label: text(row?.label),
    detail: text(row?.detail),
    stars: flag(row?.stars),
  }))
  .filter(
    (row) =>
      row.value !== "" ||
      row.unit !== "" ||
      row.label !== "" ||
      row.detail !== "",
  );

/* The token map, hover table and resolvers live in ./cta.ts — ONE copy for
   every block that draws a CTA button, extracted the day the fifth copy
   appeared. The tables only work because the blocks agree about what "book"
   resolves to, and a sixth copy here is a sixth place for the next hover-label
   edit to miss. */
const cta = resolveCta(ctaHref, ctaHover);
const ctaLabelText = text(ctaLabel);
const hasCta = ctaLabelText !== "";

const hasCopy =
  eyebrowText !== "" ||
  headingHtml !== "" ||
  bodyText !== "" ||
  body2HeadingText !== "" ||
  body2Text !== "" ||
  body3HeadingText !== "" ||
  body3Text !== "" ||
  hasCta;
const hasStats = rows.length > 0;

/**
 * A block with nothing in it draws NOTHING — no `<section>`, no `.why-grid`,
 * not one empty div.
 *
 * This is stricter than StatCalloutBlock, which always draws its shell, and the
 * asymmetry is deliberate: that block's shell is a `.section` with 110px of
 * padding top and bottom, and an all-blank row of THIS layout would put 220px
 * of coloured band on a live client page with not a word inside it. An editor
 * who adds a row and saves before filling it in would publish that gap without
 * ever seeing the build log (vs-deploy.php:142-158). Nothing is the only
 * rendering of nothing that cannot look broken.
 */
const hasAnything = hasCopy || hasStats;
/* WHY THE SHELL DRAWS EVEN WHEN EVERY FIELD IS BLANK, though drawing nothing
   reads as the safer choice: the eight registered blocks all emit
   `<section><div class="wrap"></div></section>` for an empty row, and two of
   the four blocks added alongside this one do too. A block that emits NOTHING
   while its row still carries an anchor and a nav_label puts a chip in the
   "On this page" rail pointing at an id nothing rendered — and worse, the rail
   loops count every non-blank anchor, so the missing id drifts the `-2`
   dedupe suffixes of every anchor after it. One inconsistent block is a whole
   page's rail. Consistency with the siblings is the correctness argument here,
   not tidiness. */

---

<section class:list={["section", bandModifier]} id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>{
  hasAnything && (
      <div class="why-grid">
        {hasCopy && (
          <div class="why-copy">
            {eyebrowText && <span class="eyebrow">{eyebrowText}</span>}
            {
              /* The `<Fragment>` stays on the `<h2>`'s own line rather than
                 being indented inside it, and this comment stays INSIDE the
                 braces: all three templates write the tag as one line, and a
                 line break inside it becomes a space on either side of the
                 heading — invisible to a reader, a byte either side of every
                 heading this block draws. Same one-line form as
                 StatCalloutBlock:283. A comment on its own line would be a
                 second pair of whitespace text nodes, which is the same
                 mistake in a different place (MediaSplitBlock:1046). */
              headingHtml && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>
            }
            {bodyText && <p>{bodyText}</p>}{
              /* Flush against the previous conditional, no newline between the
                 two braces. Astro preserves whitespace between expressions and
                 compressHTML collapses it to a single space rather than
                 removing it, so a conditional on its own line adds a permanent
                 space to EVERY row where the field is blank — including
                 cosmetic's, which has no `<h3>` at all. Same idiom as
                 StatCalloutBlock:224 and CardGridBlock:369. */
              body2HeadingText && <h3>{body2HeadingText}</h3>
            }{
              /* Same flush idiom, same reason: implant and general fill this,
                 and a row that fills neither must render byte-for-byte as its
                 template does today. */
              body2Text && <p>{body2Text}</p>
            }{
              /* Third pair, same flush idiom and the same reason. ESCAPED, not
                 `set:html`, because that is what `body2` two lines up does:
                 giving siblings different escaping is a trap for whoever fills
                 them next. All four saved body_3 values are markup-free prose,
                 so nothing is lost today; rich text here is a change to BOTH. */
              body3HeadingText && <h3>{body3HeadingText}</h3>
            }{
              body3Text && <p>{body3Text}</p>
            }{
              /* And again for the button. No `.cta-row` and no `.cta-buttons`
                 wrapper: the three pages put `<Button>` straight into
                 `.why-copy` (implant-dentistry/index.astro:288) and the gap
                 above it is `.why-copy p:last-of-type`, not a row's margin. */
              hasCta && (
                <Button variant="ghost" href={cta.href} hoverLabel={cta.hover}>
                  {ctaLabelText}
                </Button>
              )
            }
          </div>
        )}
        {hasStats && (
          <div class="why-stats">
            {rows.map((row) => (
              <div class="stat-card">
                {(row.value !== "" || row.unit !== "") && (
                  <div class="stat-num">{row.value}{row.unit && <em>{row.unit}</em>}</div>
                )}
                {row.label && <div class="stat-label">{row.label}</div>}
                {(row.detail !== "" || row.stars) && (
                  /* The star run, its plain space and its `&nbsp;` all sit on
                     ONE line inside the paragraph. Breaking them across lines
                     would put a newline between `</span>` and the entity, and
                     another between the entity and the detail — two extra
                     spaces in the only card per page that carries stars, and
                     one leading space in every card that does not.

                     The glyphs are a LITERAL text node in this template, the
                     same way MediaSplitBlock:822 writes them, not `{row.stars}`
                     — the field is a flag and printing it would put whatever an
                     editor or a route map happened to type on the page. The
                     space between `</span>` and the `<Fragment set:html>` is
                     load-bearing and it survives BECAUSE both sides are
                     elements: MediaSplitBlock:812 records that the compiler
                     collapses a bare space between an EXPRESSION and an element
                     on one line, which is why the run is built from elements
                     rather than from `{" "}`. Verified in the rendered output,
                     `</span> &nbsp;Consistent…`, not assumed.

                     A card with `stars` on and `detail` blank therefore ends on
                     the `&nbsp;` — seen and left, not missed. It is an
                     invisible trailing space inside a `<p>`, no page in the
                     corpus ships that shape, and splitting the run into two
                     conditionals to avoid it would put a second expression
                     boundary in the one place whose whitespace this whole
                     comment exists to protect. Cheap to change if a page ever
                     wants it; not worth the risk today. */
                  <p class="stat-detail">{row.stars && <Fragment><span class="stars" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span> <Fragment set:html={NBSP_HTML} /></Fragment>}{row.detail}</p>
                )}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
  )
}</section>

<style>
  /* ── CSS REACHABILITY, the rule this file is written against ──────────────
     Astro 6.1.9 compiles ATTRIBUTE scoping and applies the attribute to EVERY
     compound in a selector (verified in dist/_assets/PageBlocks.*.css:
     `.compare-card[data-astro-cid-…] ul[data-astro-cid-…] li[data-astro-cid-…]`).
     So a two-part scoped selector lands at (0,4,0) and beats the page sheets'
     (0,2,0)/(0,3,0) rules outright — but a ONE-part selector, `.why-grid[cid]`,
     lands at (0,2,0) and merely TIES `.impd .why-grid`, leaving bundle order to
     decide. Nothing below is written as a single compound for that reason:
     every rule that a page sheet also writes is nested at least one level
     deeper than the page's own copy of it.

     Two page rules are higher than they look and were measured, not guessed:
     `.impd .section.alt h3` is (0,3,1) — `.section.alt` is TWO classes — which
     is why the charcoal band, not `.impd .why-copy h3`, is what actually paints
     that heading white on the live page (Chrome DevTools Protocol
     CSS.getMatchedStylesForNode against dist/implant-dentistry/index.html). And
     global.css:281-330's white-card rescue is (0,4,1) on `.stat-card p`, so the
     card's own rules below are written three levels deep to clear it. Every
     colour in this file is the value the built page actually computes, not the
     value its stylesheet appears to declare. */

  /* ── Band shell ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     implant-dentistry.css:307-308 (padding), :310-311 and :333-334 (the alt and
     dark backgrounds); cream and sage complete the five-band map and are
     StatCalloutBlock's, unchanged.

     `background` on the base rule is this block's own: `.impd .section` sets
     none because the page body is already paper. A block that can be dropped on
     any page has to paint its own ground (R3).

     `.section[cid]` ties `.impd .section` at (0,2,0), and `.section[cid].alt`
     ties `.impd .section.alt` at (0,3,0). Both ties are harmless BECAUSE the
     values are identical on both sides — 110px 0, and --vs-charcoal-green — so
     no bundle order can render them differently. That is the only kind of tie
     this file allows, and it is the same one StatCalloutBlock:352 lives with. */
  .section {
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream { background: var(--vs-cream); }
  .section.dark { background: var(--vs-sage-pale); }
  .section.sage { background: var(--vs-sage); }
  .section.alt { background: var(--vs-charcoal-green); }

  /* Keep the heading clear of the stacked global Nav + sticky TOC when a rail
     chip is clicked — implant-dentistry.css:300-303. Both custom properties are
     written onto the page wrapper at runtime and inherit down; the fallbacks
     are the desktop measurements. */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + var(--toc-h, 68px) + 12px);
  }

  /* ── The split ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     implant-dentistry.css:420-428, character for character (general:438-446,
     cosmetic:274-278 are the same rule under their own prefixes).

     `.why-grid` IS the constrained container here — there is no `.wrap` inside
     this band on any of the three pages, so the 1240px cap and the 40px gutter
     live on the grid itself. Nested under `.section` to clear the tie described
     at the top of this block. */
  .section .why-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.15fr 1fr;
    gap: 64px;
    align-items: center;
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* ── The copy column ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The eyebrow is the page primitive (implant-dentistry.css:54-61) plus this
     band's own inline-flex rule (:429-433). Nested three deep so it clears
     `.impd .section.alt .eyebrow` (0,4,0), which is what actually colours it on
     the live page. */
  .why-grid .why-copy .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
